   132	## 3. Part (2): the bottom-disc expansion of the Keller condition
   133	
   134	### 3.1 The chart, and the exact order decomposition
   135	
   136	Put `t = x^{-1}`, `ord = ord_t`, and let `σ_1` be the truncation at radius `δ_1` of a
   137	`g`-root of a bottom-major disc `D_1`. In the chart
   138	
   139	```text
   140	        y = σ_1(x) + π x^{-δ_1},        (x,y) → (x,π) has Jacobian  ∂π/∂y = x^{δ_1},
   141	```
   142	
   143	write `f = x^A Φ(x,π)`, `g = x^B Γ(x,π)` with
   144	
   145	```text
   146	   A := −λ_f(δ_1) = m(1−δ_1)/(n+m) = d κ,   B := −λ_g(δ_1) = n(1−δ_1)/(n+m) = e κ,
   147	   κ := (1−δ_1)/(d+e),        A + B − 1 + δ_1 = 0.
   148	```
   149	
   150	The last identity is **exactly D1-PIN**: `A + B = 1 − δ_1` is `floor = ceiling` at `r = 1`.
   151	Because the `σ'`-terms cancel in the 2×2 determinant,
   152	
   153	```text
   154	   (♦)   [f,g] = A Φ Γ_π − B Φ_π Γ + x(Φ_x Γ_π − Φ_π Γ_x)  =  c .
   155	```
   156	
   157	Expanding `Φ = Σ_ν Φ_ν x^{-ν}`, `Γ = Σ_ν Γ_ν x^{-ν}` (`ν ≥ 0` in the value group of the
   158	branch; `x∂_x` acts as `−ν`), the coefficient of `x^{-ν}` in (♦) is
   159	
   160	```text
   161	   Σ_{α+β=ν} [ (A−α) Φ_α Γ_β' − (B−β) Φ_α' Γ_β ]  =  c·[ν = 0].
   162	```
   163	
   164	Write `P := Γ_0 = p_g` (degree `a_1 = eV_2`) and `Q := Φ_0 = p_f` (degree `b_1 = dV_2`).
   165	Then
   166	
   167	```text
   168	   ORDER 0    A Q P' − B Q' P = c                                            (BOTTOM)
   169	   ORDER ν>0  L_ν(Φ_ν, Γ_ν) = −R_ν,
   170	              L_ν(F,G) := (A−ν) F P' − B F' P + A Q G' − (B−ν) Q' G,
   171	              R_ν := Σ_{α+β=ν, α,β>0} [ (A−α)Φ_αΓ_β' − (B−β)Φ_α'Γ_β ].
   172	```
   173	
   174	Dividing by `κ` and setting `ν̃ := ν/κ`, `L_ν` becomes a **skeleton-free** operator:
   175	
   176	```text
   177	      L̃_ν̃(F,G) = (d − ν̃) F P' − e F' P + d Q G' − (e − ν̃) Q' G .
   178	```
   179	
   180	*Where the time function enters.* JAC-FIBRE in fibre form is
   181	`d/dx f(x,τ_i(x)) = c/g_y(x,τ_i(x))`, and `f/(g−c_2) = Σ_i F_i/(g_y(τ_i)(y−τ_i))` turns the
   182	charge's condition (i) into `Σ_i F_i F_i' τ_i^r = 0` for `r = 0,…,n−m−2` and `= c` at
   183	`r = n−m−1`; at `r = 0` that reads `d/dx[Σ_i F_i(x)²] = 0` — the trace of `f²` over the
   184	generic fibre is constant in `x`, a conservation law for the Keller time function. These
   185	are *automatic* for a polynomial `f`; the content is the **converse**, that the `F_i`
   186	produced by integrating `g` alone assemble into a polynomial. That converse localises to
   187	(♦): (♦) at every order **is** the full Jacobian condition read at the bottom disc.
   188	
   189	### 3.2 Order 0 is Moh Prop 4.6 at `r = 1`, and it never kills
   190	
   191	Moh's Definition 4.1 (moh.txt:1326) is `D(a,b,p,q) = a p q' − b q p'`, and Prop 4.6's last
   192	clause (moh.txt:1637) reads, verbatim,
   193	
   194	> Moreover if `r = 1` then `g_σ(π)` and `T^ψ_{1,σ}(π)` satisfy the following differential
   195	> equation `D(n, −M_1, g_σ(π), T^ψ_{1,σ}(π)) = nonzero constant.`
   196	
   197	With `−M_1 = m` and `A : B = m : n = d : e`, `(BOTTOM)` **is** that clause up to sign:
   198	
   199	```text
   200	      (BOTTOM)     d Q P' − e Q' P  =  γ ≠ 0 ,     deg P = eV_2,  deg Q = dV_2 .
   201	```
   202	
   203	> **PROPOSITION TF-0 (PROVED-HERE).** `(BOTTOM)` with `γ ≠ 0` forces `P` and `Q` to have
   204	> **simple roots** and to be **coprime**.
   205	>
   206	> *Proof.* At a double root `π_0` of `P`, `P(π_0)=P'(π_0)=0` so `γ = 0`; likewise for `Q`;
   207	> at a common root both terms vanish. ∎ (machine-checked, `tfcal.py`.)
   208	
   209	This is a second, one-line proof of **D1-STAR (d)** — and it also settles the `f`-family:
   210	the review's "intra-`f` clustering below `δ_1` is free" is **refuted**, `Q` has simple roots.
   211	
   212	> **PROPOSITION TF-DESSIN (PROVED-HERE).** Put `φ := Q^e/P^d` (well defined, `deg φ = deV`,
   213	> `φ(∞) = 1`). Then `φ'/φ = −γ/(PQ)`, so `φ` is ramified only over `{0,1,∞}` with profile
   214	> ```text
   215	>    over ∞ :  eV points of index d            over 0 :  dV points of index e
   216	>    over 1 :  ONE point (π = ∞) of index (d+e)V − 1, all others simple;
   217	>              deg(Q^e − P^d) = deV − (d+e)V + 1 .
   218	> ```
   219	> Equivalently: a genus-0 bipartite map with `dV` black vertices of degree `e`, `eV` white
   220	> of degree `d`, one face of degree `(d+e)V−1` and `V(de−d−e)+1` faces of degree 1.
   221	
   222	For `(d,e) = (2,3)` the white vertices have degree 2 and contract, leaving a **cubic plane
   223	map** with `2V` vertices, `3V` edges, `V+1` monogonal faces and one face of degree `5V−1`.
   224	Deleting the `V+1` loops leaves a tree with `2V` vertices, `V+1` leaves and `V−1` trivalent
   225	vertices — which exists for every `V ≥ 1` and is planar; re-attaching one loop at each leaf
   226	reconstructs the map. By Riemann existence, `(BOTTOM)` is **solvable for every `V`**.
   227	Machine witnesses (`star_explicit.py`, all clauses verified: constant, nonzero, simple
   228	roots both sides, coprime, `deg(Q^e−P^d)` as predicted):
   229	
   230	```text
   231	   V = 1 :  P = π³ + 3π,                 Q = π² + 2,      γ = 12
   232	   V = 2 :  P = π⁶ + 3π³ + 3/2,          Q = π⁴ + 2π,     γ = −9
   233	```
   234	
   235	(The `V = 2` solution is the `ζ_3`-equivariant one the `K_{1,3}` dessin predicts.)
   236	Unknown/equation count `((d+e)V+2` vs `(d+e)V−2)` matches the 4-dimensional symmetry group
   237	`{π ↦ απ+β} × {P ↦ λP} × {Q ↦ μQ}`, so the solution is rigid modulo symmetry — the `V = 1`
   238	solution above is the unique one.
   239	
   240	**Consequence.** `OPEN[STAR-REALISABILITY]` — "a skeleton whose bottom polynomial `p(π)` is
   241	forced to have a repeated root dies" — has the answer **no such skeleton exists**: the
   242	repeated root is never forced, at any `(d,e,V)`. The bottom leading form is not a census
   243	filter.
