   114	       In § 3 we relate the sequence of polynomials Tf with the Jacobian condition for
   115	f(x9 y) and g(x9 y). It is easy to see that the task is to study the partial derivatives
   116	 of 7J*(/, g) with respect to /. Proposition 3.3 shows that if the similarity of the dis-
   117	 tribution of roots holds for g(x9 y), Tf (f(x9 y), g(x9y)),..., T* (f(x, y), g(x, y))9 then it
   118	holds for the extra polynomials — 1^+1 (f(x, y)9 g(x, y)). It furnishes an important step
   119	in our study. From the next section on the Jacobian condition is assumed.
   120	
   121	
   122	      In §4 we study the basic Splitting phenomena and nonsplitting phenomena. In-
   123	ductively we locate a certain disc D in k^t^. The question is to determine if some roots
   124	τί9 TJ of g (i"1, y) Π Tp(f(t~l9 y), g (i"1, y)) in D have a distance which equals the radius
   125	                   i=l
   126	of D. If all distances are less than the radius of Z), then the disc D is not one of the
   127	discs which determine the tree data of the said polynomial. In this case we have a non-
   128	splitting phenomenon. To locate the tree data we have to further shrink the radius of
   129	the disc. Proposition 4.4 establishes some sufficient numerical criteria for the non-
   130	splitting phenomena. On the other band if there are some roots τ ί9 τ,· having a distance
   131	which equals the radius of /), then the disc D is clearly one of the discs which determine
   132	the tree data of the said polynomial. In this case we have a Splitting phenomenon.
   133	Proposition 4. 6 establishes some sufficient numerical criteria for the Splitting phenomenon
   134	and some Information about the size of the disc D and the number of the subdiscs of D
   135	in the next step of the decomposition in the construction of the tree of discs. Later on
   136	we implicitly establish that the two numerical criteria of Propositions 4. 4 and 4. 6 cover
   137	all possibilities.
   138	
   139	
   140	      According to the number of roots in it, the disc D can be classified s either
   141	a major disc or a minor disc. A major disc is one which contains more than a pre-
   142	determined number of roots (cf. Definition 4.1). Otherwise it is a minor one. In § 5 we
   143	study the towers of major discs. Propositions 5. l, 5.2 and 5. 3 establish the existence
   144	and the critical numerical properties of them. Namely a new sequence {vs9 vs_l9..., v2}
   145	of restricted integers which are the relative numbers of roots in a tower of major discs
   146	A13 A-i"· iDD2=>Di will determine the radii of those discs completely.
   147	
   148	
   149	       In the remaining part of § 5 we show various applications of Proposition 5. 3.
   150	Proposition 5.4 establishes that in the case of <5 f > — l (i.e. there is only one point at oo
   151	for f(x9 y)) there is a reduction of deg/(;c, y) and deg g(;c, y). Proposition 5. 5 establishes
   152	the similar result for the case of two characteristic pairs.
